{"candidates": [{"claim": "As n → ∞ the probability of unanimous consensus tends to 1, i.e. consensus occurs with probability 1 - o(1). The only finite obstructions are locally stable mixed configurations in which every vertex has at least two of its three neighbors sharing its own color; on a random 3-regular graph this forces each color class to induce a subgraph of minimum degree ≥ 2, hence a union of cycles with all edges 'internal' plus a perfect matching of crossing edges between the two induced subgraphs. The expected number of such configurations reachable from a uniform random start is not the naive static count (which is exp(Θ(n)) for the family but each member has probability 2^{-n} of being hit exactly); the dynamical trapping probability per trajectory is instead governed by a cut-descending drift: the asynchronous update rule weakly decreases the number of crossing edges at each step except when the selected vertex is already in a locally stable position, and on an expander the drift is negative and of order √n until the cut is empty. A falsifiable quantitative law: the probability of non-consensus equals Θ(c^{-n}) for an explicit c > 1 (heuristically the chance the dynamics freezes into a stable bisection before the cut empties), so consensus probability approaches 1 exponentially; simulation should show log(1 - P(consensus)) linear in n.", "counterconditions": ["If the cut-descending drift can be non-negative on a positive fraction of trajectory steps without local stability, the basin argument fails and consensus probability may stay bounded away from 1.", "If reachable stable matchings have total basin weight bounded below by a constant (SRC_006's stance), consensus probability tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1, refuting this candidate.", "If one can exhibit even a Θ(1/n) fraction of random colorings whose descent terminates in a stable matching on a random 3-regular graph, the exponential law is falsified.", "If vertices on induced cycles with crossing chords can flip (SRC_013's refutation condition), the obstruction characterization must be narrowed and the drift argument revisited."], "discharges": [{"handle": "SRC_021", "kind": "revised", "note": "This candidate states an explicit scaling law: P(non-consensus) = Θ(c^{-n}), falsifiable via log-linearity in n, satisfying pa1-scaling-law.", "where": "claim, second half"}, {"handle": "SRC_020", "kind": "revised", "note": "Explicit limit verdict: consensus probability tends to 1 (1 - o(1)) as n grows.", "where": "claim, first sentence"}, {"handle": "SRC_017", "kind": "revised", "note": "Quantitative prevalence law included: expected trapped-trajectory fraction decays like c^{-n}.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_016", "kind": "revised", "note": "Obstruction prevalence scaling law given (exponential decay of trapping probability) rather than a bare Poisson count.", "where": "claim, final sentence"}, {"handle": "SRC_005", "kind": "rebutted", "note": "The criticism says absence of single-edge stable obstructions initially does not decide consensus. Rebutted: this candidate never argues from the initial coloring's static stability; it argues from the dynamics' drift, and the initial instability is precisely what fuels the negative cut-drift toward the empty cut.", "where": "mechanism"}, {"handle": "SRC_023", "kind": "revised", "note": "See scaling law: exponential decay constant c, measurable in simulation.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_009", "kind": "revised", "note": "Scaling law present: Θ(c^{-n}) with explicit falsifiable simulation signature.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_004", "kind": "revised", "note": "Limit verdict explicitly stated with asymptotic language.", "where": "claim"}], "mechanism": "Asynchronous majority update on a 3-regular graph is a monotone descent on the cut functional: a selected vertex flips exactly when a majority of its neighbors disagrees with it, and for degree 3 a flipping vertex strictly reduces the number of crossing edges (a degree-3 vertex with ≥2 opposite-colored neighbors loses at least 2 crossing edges and gains at most 1). So the number of crossing edges is a non-increasing integer potential, decreasing by at least 1 at every genuine flip. Absorption happens either at the empty cut (consensus) or at a 'frozen' configuration where every vertex has ≥2 same-colored neighbors — i.e. both color classes induce min-degree ≥ 2 subgraphs connected by a perfect matching of crossing edges (a stable matching-cut, refining SRC_014). On a random 3-regular graph, which is an expander with high probability, a random initial cut has Θ(n) crossing edges and the local drift is strongly negative: vertices with 2 or 3 opposite neighbors vastly outnumber frozen vertices at any cut size ≥ εn, so the process whp descends to the empty cut before ever sampling a stable matching-cut. The set of stable matching-cuts reachable before the cut empties has total measure that decays exponentially in n on an expander, because each requires an induced min-degree-2 bipartition of a subgraph whose structure is quantitatively rigid (counts of induced 2-regular subgraphs on random cubic graphs are exponentially suppressed relative to all cuts).", "optional_refs": ["SRC_007", "SRC_010", "SRC_014"], "sidecar": {"requested_context_aliases": ["SRC_006", "SRC_013"], "search_signal": "productive"}, "typicality": 0.7}, {"claim": "As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1 — heuristically ≈ 1/2, more precisely equal to the fraction of trajectories whose cut-descent ends at the empty cut rather than at a stable matching-cut, a fraction that converges because the descent is dominated by a constant-probability early-phase bottleneck. The finite obstructions are exactly the stable matching-cuts: mixed configurations where each color class induces a spanning subgraph of minimum degree ≥ 2 and the two classes are joined by the remaining perfect matching. Quantitative law (falsifiable): the number of stable matching-cuts on a random 3-regular graph is Θ(2^{n/2}) asymptotically almost surely, but the trapping basin weight is the operative quantity, and it converges to a constant p* ∈ (0,1) independent of n; simulation of the empirical consensus frequency should show convergence to a n-independent limit with O(1/√n) fluctuations and no exponential or linear-in-n trend.", "counterconditions": ["If the trapping basin weight decays to 0 (exponentially or polynomially), consensus probability tends to 1 and this candidate is refuted.", "If the trapping basin weight tends to 1, consensus probability tends to 0 and this candidate is refuted.", "If simulation shows the empirical consensus frequency drifting with n at any scale, the claimed n-independence fails.", "If stable matching-cuts do not exist on almost all random cubic graphs (girth/parity obstructions), the obstruction characterization collapses."], "discharges": [{"handle": "SRC_021", "kind": "revised", "note": "Scaling law now explicit: Θ(2^{n/2}) count of obstructions and constant basin weight p*, with Θ(1/√n) fluctuation scale.", "where": "claim, second half"}, {"handle": "SRC_020", "kind": "revised", "note": "Limit verdict: tends to a constant strictly between 0 and 1.", "where": "claim, first clause"}, {"handle": "SRC_017", "kind": "revised", "note": "Quantitative law stated with explicit count and basin weight.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_016", "kind": "revised", "note": "Count of stable matching-cuts given as Θ(2^{n/2}) a.a.s., satisfying the scaling-law criterion.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_005", "kind": "revised", "note": "Conceded: static Poisson counting of obstructions in the initial coloring does not settle consensus. This candidate instead bases the verdict on the dynamical basin weight, exactly the distinction the criticism demands.", "where": "mechanism"}, {"handle": "SRC_023", "kind": "revised", "note": "See Θ(2^{n/2}) and constant p*.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_009", "kind": "revised", "note": "Scaling law present with falsifiable simulation signature (no drift in n).", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_004", "kind": "revised", "note": "Limit verdict explicit.", "where": "claim"}], "mechanism": "The cut-size functional descends monotonically (each genuine degree-3 majority flip removes ≥1 crossing edge), so the state space decomposes into basins of two kinds of absorbing states: the two consensus states (empty cut) and stable matching-cuts. Because the initial random coloring has Θ(n) crossing edges and the descent performs Θ(n) flips total, the process has Θ(n) chances to 'fall into' a stable matching-cut. Each stable matching-cut corresponds to choosing one of the Θ(√2)^n near-bisections whose induced subgraphs are min-degree ≥ 2; on a random cubic graph with high girth the local rigidity is high but so is the multiplicity, and the per-step trapping probability stabilizes to an n-independent constant (the local structure seen by the descent is asymptotically tree-like, by weak local convergence of random cubic graphs to the 3-regular tree). Since the descent on a tree-like local view has a fixed chance q per unit of cut decrease to hit a frozen local pattern, and the number of 'attempts' is proportional to initial cut size with the per-attempt success being proportional to 1/(attempts) — the two linear-in-n effects cancel — the total trapped fraction converges to a nondegenerate constant strictly between 0 and 1. This is the same cancellation SRC_013 gestures at (Θ(n) opportunities vs per-edge probability p_n), but resolved as convergence rather than decay or offset.", "optional_refs": ["SRC_006", "SRC_011", "SRC_013"], "sidecar": {"search_signal": "productive"}, "typicality": 0.45}, {"claim": "Atypical: the question as posed has no well-defined single limit, because the consensus probability on random 3-regular graphs does not converge as n grows — it oscillates with the parity of n and the prevalence of short cycles, so none of 'tends to 1', 'tends to 0', 'strictly between' is correct without restricting to a subsequence. The obstruction structures are stable matching-cuts (each color class inducing min-degree ≥ 2, joined by the complementary perfect matching), and the falsifiable quantitative law is: the expected number of stable matching-cuts equals Θ(n^{1/2} · λ^n) for a base λ with 1 < λ < √2, but the probability that the dynamics is trapped scales as a non-monotone function of n oscillating by parity, because stable matching-cuts require each color class to have an even number of degree-2-and-3 vertices summing appropriately, giving a parity-dependent factor of up to 2 between n-even and n-odd subsequences. Simulation over consecutive n should show a persistent parity gap in consensus frequency that does not shrink.", "counterconditions": ["If simulation shows the parity gap in consensus frequency vanishing with n, the non-convergence claim is falsified and a genuine limit verdict should be preferred.", "If the expected count of stable matching-cuts is Θ(2^n) rather than λ^n with λ < √2, the obstruction census here is wrong.", "If stable matching-cuts cannot exist on random cubic graphs of odd girth parity, oscillation must come from elsewhere or disappear."], "discharges": [{"handle": "SRC_021", "kind": "revised", "note": "Scaling law stated: Θ(n^{1/2} λ^n) count, plus parity-oscillating trapping probability.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_020", "kind": "departure_declared", "note": "This candidate departs from the trichotomy: it declares no single limit exists, hence no 'tends to' verdict is offered — the limit verdict is replaced by a non-convergence claim.", "where": "claim, first clause"}, {"handle": "SRC_017", "kind": "revised", "note": "Explicit count law Θ(n^{1/2}·λ^n).", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_016", "kind": "revised", "note": "Expected number of stable matching-cuts given with explicit asymptotics.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_005", "kind": "departure_declared", "note": "Departing from the framing that static obstruction counts in the initial coloring decide anything: this candidate uses the parity structure of reachable obstructions only.", "where": "mechanism"}, {"handle": "SRC_023", "kind": "revised", "note": "λ^n scaling with parity oscillation is the quantitative law.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_009", "kind": "revised", "note": "Scaling law present.", "where": "claim"}, {"handle": "SRC_004", "kind": "departure_declared", "note": "No limit verdict; declared departure from the trichotomy framing.", "where": "claim"}], "mechanism": "Stable mixed configurations on a cubic graph are exactly bisections whose induced subgraphs both have minimum degree ≥ 2; each color class is then a union of cycles with attached degree-3 chords. The combinatorial census of such bisections on random cubic graphs is sensitive to the cycle structure of the graph, which itself has known parity and short-cycle fluctuations (Poisson number of cycles of each fixed length, and a global parity constraint on perfect matchings of the crossing-edge structure — every stable matching-cut uses a perfect matching of the graph). Since perfect matchings of cubic graphs interact with bridge structure and parity of component sizes, the count of stable matching-cuts inherits a factor oscillating with n mod small integers. Because the consensus probability equals the basin weight split between the empty cut and these obstructions, and obstruction prevalence oscillates by parity while its mean decays only mildly, the consensus probability inherits the oscillation and does not converge. The neighbourhood's three lenses (decay / stabilize / offset) each hold on a different subsequence.", "optional_refs": ["SRC_012", "SRC_013"], "sidecar": {"search_signal": "productive"}, "typicality": 0.15}]}